<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  FastDDS profile for the FLEET: shared memory for data, UDPv4 for discovery.

  WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE fastdds_udp_only.xml.

  That profile turns shared memory OFF, and its own note explains why it was
  safe to: "Everything here is on one host over loopback, so the throughput cost
  is not a concern." That was written for ONE robot, and for one robot it is
  true. It stops being true at three.

  Measured with three robots running the delivery job:

      /tf                76 Hz, 419 transforms/s, 29 SUBSCRIBERS
      robot_state_publisher   53% of a core EACH
      ekf_node                56% of a core EACH
      one-minute load         39 on 16 cores

  Without shared memory every one of those /tf messages is serialised and sent
  twenty-nine separate times through the loopback UDP stack, and the same is
  true of each robot's 480x480 RGB-D point cloud. The receivers then deserialise
  every copy. That is why nodes which compute almost nothing -- forward
  kinematics, a fifteen-state filter -- were burning half a core apiece, and why
  Nav2's own nodes started timing out acknowledging each other's goals.

  SHM is listed FIRST so local traffic takes it; UDPv4 stays listed because
  shared memory cannot carry multicast discovery, and because a participant that
  cannot reach a peer over SHM needs a way through.

  THE FAILURE THAT ORIGINALLY MOTIVATED DISABLING SHM IS REAL, and worth knowing
  how to clear rather than avoiding forever:

      RTPS_TRANSPORT_SHM Error: Failed init_port fastrtps_port<N>:
          open_and_lock_file failed

  It comes from stale segment files left in /dev/shm by processes that were
  killed rather than shut down -- which, in this project, is every teardown. The
  remedy is to remove them while no ROS process is running:

      rm -f /dev/shm/fastrtps_* /dev/shm/sem.fastrtps_*

  If a participant ever hangs on "Waiting messages on topic
  [/robot_description]", that is the symptom, and the line above is the fix.
  Falling back to config/fastdds_udp_only.xml also works and costs only speed.

  The segment is sized for the biggest thing that goes through it: a 480x480
  organised point cloud is about 2.5 MB, and several can be in flight at once.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>shm_transport</transport_id>
        <type>SHM</type>
        <segment_size>67108864</segment_size>
      </transport_descriptor>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>udp_transport</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="shm_and_udp_participant" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>shm_transport</transport_id>
          <transport_id>udp_transport</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
  </profiles>
</dds>
